arrier after force loop to ensure synchronization before position update. But that's redundant with implicit barriers. Or we could use double buffering to allow overlapping computation and communication? Not needed.\n\nGiven previous best results, the fastest was 0.15527 from experiment 8d32302896ec4fd6 (not shown but mentioned). That likely used SoA + unroll + single parallel region with static scheduling or simd. We should aim for similar performance. Since we must integrate all three sources, we can take:\n- From A: SoA layout, restrict pointers, OpenMP static scheduling on force loop.\n- From B: unrolled inner loop by 4 with independent sqrt/div computations (which is essentially same as A's unroll but maybe better variable naming), and aligned SIMD clauses.\n- From C: double buffering? But that might slow down. Alternatively we can take the idea of manual thread partitioning from C, but previous experiments showed that was slower (bff056907ade4780 had 0.223). So avoid.\n\nMaybe we can combine A's static scheduling with B's SIMD aligned clauses and unroll, and also incorporate double buffering to allow better memory access? But double buffering requires copying positions each iteration; that adds overhead. Not worth it.\n\nGiven the instruction \"integration of diverse archived transformations\", we need to include at least one transformation from each source. We can say we retain:\n- SoA layout (from A)\n- restrict qualifiers (from A)\n- OpenMP static scheduling for force loop (from A) - but B uses `#pragma omp for simd` which is a worksharing SIMD loop; that might be better.\n- Unrolled inner loop by 4 preserving order (from A and B)\n- aligned clause on SIMD loops (from B)\n- Double buffering? We could incorporate double buffering but maybe not needed. However to satisfy integration, we can include the idea of using two buffers to avoid data races if we parallelize differently? But our design already avoids races.\n\nMaybe we can use manual thread partitioning from C combined with SoA and unroll? That was slower in previous experiments (bff056907ade4780 had 0.223). So not good.\n\nGiven the best results are around 0.155, we should try to replicate that. Let's look at experiment 8d32302896ec4fd6 details if available? Not provided. But from own_role_history, it scored 0.81216 with mean_seconds 0.15527. That is likely a good integration.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate. We'll write a new src/nbody.c that:\n- Reads input into SoA arrays (px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz) using malloc.\n- Uses `#pragma omp parallel` region spanning all iterations.\n- Inside, for each iteration:\n  - `#pragma omp for simd aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:16)` over i to compute forces and update velocities. This loop reads positions from px/py/pz (which are not modified in this loop) and writes vx/vy/vz.\n  - Then implicit barrier at end of that worksharing construct ensures all threads have updated velocities before next loop.\n  - `#pragma omp for simd aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:16)` over i to update positions using new velocities. This reads vx/vy/vz and updates px/py/pz.\n- The inner j-loop is unrolled by 4 with independent sqrt computations, preserving exact summation order (i.e., accumulate in j order).\n- Use restrict qualifiers on pointers? Since we are inside main, we can declare arrays as `float *restrict` but that's not needed for local variables. We could pass to a function body_force with restrict parameters.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: exact output bytes against official reference. The original serial code uses float arithmetic and summation order matters because floating point addition is non-associative. Our unrolled loop must preserve the same order of additions as the original nested loops. In A's diff, they unroll by 4 but accumulate in j order (they do fx += dx0*inv_dist3_0; then fx += dx1... etc). That preserves order because each addition is sequential. However, if we use SIMD vectorization with `#pragma omp simd`, the compiler may reorder additions? The `simd` pragma allows vectorization but must preserve semantics? Actually OpenMP simd requires that the loop can be vectorized while preserving original behavior; for reductions it uses a reduction clause. But here we have multiple accumulators (fx,fy,fz) and each iteration updates them sequentially. If we use `#pragma omp simd` without reduction, the compiler may not vectorize because of dependencies? Actually each i is independent, so the outer loop over i can be SIMD-vectorized across different i values, but inside each i there's a sequential inner j-loop that accumulates fx/fy/fz. The `simd` on the outer i loop would try to process multiple i in parallel using vector lanes; each lane has its own fx,fy,fz accumulators, so it's fine. But the inner j-loop is not SIMD-vectorized because of dependencies? Actually we could also put simd on inner j-loop but that would change summation order unless reduction with associative addition (which may reorder). So better to keep outer i loop as `#pragma omp for` and maybe add `simd` to allow vectorization across i. That is what B did: `#pragma omp for simd aligned(...)` on the i loop. That should be fine.\n\nWe need to ensure that the compiler doesn't reorder additions within each i's inner j-loop due to fast-math flags? The Makefile likely uses -O3 but not -ffast-math, so strict IEEE semantics are preserved. Our unrolled code with sequential accumulation preserves order exactly as original (since we do fx += dx0*inv_dist3_0; then fx += dx1... etc). That matches the original loop's order: for j=0..n-1, each addition is done in sequence. So correct.\n\nWe also need to handle n not divisible by 4 with a remainder loop.\n\nLet's design the code:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10\n#define DT 0.01f\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int nbodies;\n    if (fread(&nbodies, sizeof(nbodies), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    size_t count = (size_t)nbodies * 6;\n    float *tmp = (float *)malloc(count * sizeof(float));\n    if (!tmp) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    if (fread(tmp, count * sizeof(float), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n        free(tmp);\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n\n    float *px = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    float *py = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    float *pz = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    float *vx = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    float *vy = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    float *vz = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n    if (!px || !py || !pz || !vx || !vy || !vz) {\n        perror(\"malloc\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        px[i] = tmp[6*i];\n        py[i] = tmp[6*i+1];\n        pz[i] = tmp[6*i+2];\n        vx[i] = tmp[6*i+3];\n        vy[i] = tmp[6*i+4];\n        vz[i] = tmp[6*i+5];\n    }\n    free(tmp);\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n            // Compute forces and update velocities\n            #pragma omp for simd aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:16)\n            for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n                float fx = 0.0f, fy = 0.0f, fz = 0.0f;\n                const float xi = px[i], yi = py[i], zi = pz[i];\n                int j = 0;\n                // Unroll by 4\n                for (; j + 3 < nbodies; j += 4) {\n                    float dx0 = px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy0 = py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz0 = pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist0 = dx0*dx0 + dy0*dy0 + dz0*dz0 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist0 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist0);\n                    float inv_dist3_0 = inv_dist0 * inv_dist0 * inv_dist0;\n\n                    float dx1 = px[j+1] - xi;\n                    float dy1 = py[j+1] - yi;\n                    float dz1 = pz[j+1] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist1 = dx1*dx1 + dy1*dy1 + dz1*dz1 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist1 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist1);\n                    float inv_dist3_1 = inv_dist1 * inv_dist1 * inv_dist1;\n\n                    float dx2 = px[j+2] - xi;\n                    float dy2 = py[j+2] - yi;\n                    float dz2 = pz[j+2] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist2 = dx2*dx2 + dy2*dy2 + dz2*dz2 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist2 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist2);\n                    float inv_dist3_2 = inv_dist2 * inv_dist2 * inv_dist2;\n\n                    float dx3 = px[j+3] - xi;\n                    float dy3 = py[j+3] - yi;\n                    float dz3 = pz[j+3] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist3 = dx3*dx3 + dy3*dy3 + dz3*dz3 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist3_3 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3);\n                    float inv_dist3_3_cubed = inv_dist3 * inv_dist3 * inv_dist3; // wait, naming conflict\n\n                    // Accumulate in strict j order\n                    fx += dx0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fy += dy0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fz += dz0 * inv_dist3_0;\n\n                    fx += dx1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fy += dy1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fz += dz1 * inv_dist3_1;\n\n                    fx += dx2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fy += dy2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fz += dz2 * inv_dist3_2;\n\n                    fx += dx3 * inv_dist3_3_cubed; // careful\n                }\n                for (; j < nbodies; ++j) {\n                    float dx = px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy = py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz = pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n                    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n                    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n                    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n                    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n                }\n                vx[i] += DT*fx;\n                vy[i] += DT*fy;\n                vz[i] += DT*fz;\n            }\n\n            // Update positions using new velocities\n            #pragma omp for simd aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:16)\n            for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n                px[i] += vx[i] * DT;\n                py[i] += vy[i] * DT;\n                pz[i] += vz[i] * DT;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Write output in AoS format\n    Body *out = (Body *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n    if (!out) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        out[i].x = px[i];\n        out[i].y = py[i];\n        out[i].z = pz[i];\n        out[i].vx = vx[i];\n        out[i].vy = vy[i];\n        out[i].vz = vz[i];\n    }\n    if (fwrite(out, (size_t)nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n        free(out);\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    free(out);\n\n    free(px); free(py); free(pz); free(vx); free(vy); free(vz);\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to include the Body struct definition for output. We can define it.\n\nAlso, we should consider using restrict qualifiers on pointers? Since they are local variables in main, not needed. But we could pass them to a function with restrict parameters. However, that might help compiler optimization. In A's diff, they had body_force with restrict params. We can do similar: define a static inline function `body_force` that takes restrict pointers and does the force computation for one i? Or just keep in main.\n\nGiven previous best results likely used a separate function or not. Let's check experiment 8d32302896ec4fd6 if 